# STL Complexity: vector vs deque vs list

| Operation | vector | deque | list |
|---|---|---|---|
| `[i]`, `at(i)` | O(1) | O(1) | not available |
| `front()`, `back()` | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| `size()`, `empty()` | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| `push_back` / `emplace_back` | amortized O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| `pop_back` | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| `push_front` / `emplace_front` | not available | O(1) | O(1) |
| `pop_front` | not available | O(1) | O(1) |
| `insert` at front | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) |
| `insert` in middle (iterator given) | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) |
| `erase` in middle (iterator given) | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) |
| reach the i-th position | O(1) | O(1) | O(i) |
| `clear()` | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| `swap` | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| full loop | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| `std::sort(begin, end)` | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | does not compile, use `l.sort()` |

## Quick rules
- vector: fast at the back and by index, slow everywhere else.
- deque: vector, plus fast at the front.
- list: fast insert/erase anywhere once you hold the iterator, but no indexing.

## Iterator invalidation
- vector: push_back/insert may invalidate all; erase invalidates at and after the position.
- deque: insert/erase in the middle invalidates all; at either end, iterators are invalidated but references to elements stay valid.
- list: only iterators to erased elements are invalidated.
